# Recognizing a working student
#
# A working student is defined as someone who is both a student and a
# worker (owl:intersectionOf). Alice is said to be a working student; Bob is
# said to be a student and a worker. N3 rules for owl:intersectionOf derive
# that Alice is a student and a worker, and, with a log:forAllIn test over
# the class list, that Bob is a working student.

@prefix : <http://example.org/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix list: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/list#> .
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .

# The definition, and one person for each direction
:WorkingStudent owl:intersectionOf (:Student :Worker) .
:alice a :WorkingStudent .
:bob a :Student, :Worker .

# Rules for owl:intersectionOf
@prefix list: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/list#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix e: <http://eulersharp.sourceforge.net/2003/03swap/log-rules#> .

# A member of the intersection is a member of every class in the list.
{
    ?C owl:intersectionOf ?L .
    ?X a ?C .
    ?D list:in ?L
} => {
    ?X a ?D
} .

# The intersection is a subclass of each class in the list.
{
    ?C owl:intersectionOf ?L .
    ?D list:in ?L
} => {
    ?C rdfs:subClassOf ?D
} .

# A member of every class in the list is a member of the intersection. The
# first class gives a candidate; log:forAllIn then checks all of them.
{
    ?C owl:intersectionOf ?L .
    ?L list:first ?First .
    ?X a ?First .
    (
        {
            ?D list:in ?L
        }
        {
            ?X a ?D
        }
    ) log:forAllIn ?SCOPE
} => {
    ?X a ?C
} .
